7. Test Approach / Test Strategy
7.1 Test Approach
Testing will follow a risk-based and layered approach. Business-critical flows will receive priority, beginning with smoke validation of application availability and critical navigation. Functional scenarios will then be executed using positive, negative, boundary and exploratory techniques. Defect fixes will be followed by targeted retesting and regression testing.
7.2 Manual Testing
●	Requirement and acceptance-criteria validation
●	Exploratory testing
●	Usability testing
●	Ad-hoc scenarios
●	New or changed functionality
●	Negative and boundary scenarios
●	Defect verification
●	Release validation
7.3 Automation Testing
●	Playwright with TypeScript for UI automation
●	Smoke suite for build validation
●	Regression suite for stable repetitive scenarios
●	Critical end-to-end customer journeys
●	Cross-browser execution
●	Data-driven scenarios where appropriate
●	CI execution through Jenkins/GitHub Actions where configured
●	Allure/HTML reporting for execution evidence
7.4 API Testing
API testing will be performed with Postman for available and authorized endpoints. Validation will cover status codes, response structure, required fields, error handling, positive/negative inputs and basic integration behavior. API automation may be introduced if stable endpoints and authentication details are provided.
7.5 Database Testing
Database validation is conditional on approved database access. Where access is available, QA will validate critical persistence such as customer records, cart/order information and data consistency without modifying production data.
